Explanation of the change:

1. We switch the check from `node.type` to `node.body.type` so that arrows whose bodies are actually `BlockStatement`s (i.e. normal `{ … }` functions) are left untouched.
2. This prevents the fixer from wrapping block‐bodied arrows (such as the `catch(err => { … })` in your example) in parentheses and turning them into invalid object‐returning expressions.
